## What happened

In **.NET 11**, **DSA was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` (and any DSA signing/verification) now throws at runtime on macOS. Windows and Linux are unaffected — DSA still works there (though it's considered legacy).

## How to fix it

**Option 1 — Migrate to a modern algorithm** (recommended if you control the signature format):

```csharp
// Replace DSA with ECDsa
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
var signature = ecdsa.SignData(data, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

**Option 2 — Platform-guard for legacy verification only:**

If you must verify *existing* DSA signatures and can't re-sign, gate the code so it only runs on Windows/Linux:

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
    bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
}
else
{
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException(
        "DSA verification is not supported on macOS with .NET 11+.");
}
```

**Recommended path:** Since your library verifies *legacy* DSA signatures, re-sign the artifacts with **ECDSA** or **RSA** if possible. If the legacy signatures are immutable, use the platform guard and document that macOS is no longer supported for DSA verification.
